# AWS container service digest

Distilled from Amazon ECS's best practices guide, Elastic Load Balancing's
Application Load Balancer docs, DynamoDB's developer guide, the
AWS SDK for JavaScript v3 guide, the CDK developer guide and its assertions
module, and CDK Nag's rule list (see `SOURCES.md`). `[lint: x]` = enforced by
`make check`: an ESLint rule or the compiler; `[nag: x]` = a CDK Nag rule that
fails `cdk synth`; `[test]` = an assertion in `infra/standard.test.ts` or a
stack's own test; `[review]` = check it yourself. `docs/well-architected.md`
says why each rule matters.

## The server

- `src/main.ts` is the entry point the image runs: it reads the
  configuration, creates the SDK clients once, starts Node's HTTP server
  and stops it on `SIGTERM`. Nothing else runs at start-up. [review]
- Read `process.env` only in `src/main.ts`, once, and fail at start-up with
  a message naming the variable when one is missing; ECS then replaces the
  task, and the deployment's circuit breaker rolls back. [review]
- Keep `src/main.ts` to wiring; the work is in functions that take what they
  need (a store, a log), so tests call them without a socket. The server
  reads requests through the narrow `Incoming` and `Outgoing` interfaces
  `src/server.ts` defines. [review]
- Bound what a request may cost: a body limit (413 above it), and no work
  per request that can be done once at start-up. [review]
- Keep-alive longer than the load balancer's 60-second idle timeout
  (`keepAliveTimeout`, and `headersTimeout` above it), so the server never
  closes a connection the load balancer is about to reuse. [review]
- On `SIGTERM`, stop taking requests (`server.close()`), finish those in
  flight and let the process end; ECS sends `SIGKILL` after the stop
  timeout. Never `process.exit()`: it drops what is in flight.
  [lint: no-restricted-properties]
- Handle every promise: await it, or `void` it where Node's callback can't
  (the request listener), with the listener catching every failure itself.
  [lint: @typescript-eslint/no-floating-promises]
- Don't keep request data in module-scope variables: every request in the
  process would share it. [review]
- `GET /health` answers 200 while the process runs and reads nothing, so an
  outage of a dependency doesn't fail every task's health check at once.
  [review]

## Requests and responses

- Validate every input at the boundary: path parameters, query strings,
  headers and the body, narrowed from `unknown` with runtime checks. The
  load balancer delivers whatever the client sent. [review]
- Path segments arrive percent-encoded; decode them, and answer 400 when one
  doesn't decode. [review]
- Answer expected failures with their status: 400 for bad input, 403, 404,
  405 for a method a path doesn't take, 409 for a conflict, 413 for too large, and 500 only for a bug or an
  outage. The body is JSON with a `message` a client can act on. [review]
- Never return a stack trace, an SDK error's text or an internal name to a
  client; log it instead, with the request id (the load balancer's
  `X-Amzn-Trace-Id`, or one the server makes). [review]
- Set `content-type` on every response. [review]

## Logs

- Log structured JSON, one entry per line, through the `Log` the code is
  given; never `console`. [lint: no-console]
- Each entry has a `level`, a `message` and the request id; errors say what
  failed and with which input, never a secret, a token or user data. [review]
- The container logs through the `awslogs` driver to a log group the stack
  creates, with a retention. [nag: AwsSolutions-ECS7; test: log groups keep
  logs for a set time]
- Write entries to stderr, one JSON object a line; ECS sends each line to
  CloudWatch Logs as one event. [review]

## DynamoDB

- Model the table for its access patterns: every read on a request path is
  a `GetItem` or a `Query` on a key or an index, never a `Scan`. [review]
- Use the document client (`@aws-sdk/lib-dynamodb`) so items are plain
  objects; validate what a read returns before using it, since another
  writer may have stored something else. [review]
- Writes are idempotent: a `PutItem` of the whole item, or a conditional
  write (`ConditionExpression`) that refuses a second apply. Never a blind
  `ADD` on a retried request. [review]
- Tables are on demand (`PAY_PER_REQUEST`) unless the issue states a steady
  load. [review]
- Point-in-time recovery on. [nag: AwsSolutions-DDB3]
- Encrypted at rest with a KMS key (`TableEncryption.AWS_MANAGED` or
  customer-managed). [test: encrypts every table]
- The table outlives the stack: `RemovalPolicy.RETAIN` and deletion
  protection. [test: says what happens to stateful resources; review]

## The AWS SDK v3

- Import only the clients you use (`@aws-sdk/client-dynamodb`), never a
  whole SDK; esbuild bundles them into the image. [review]
- Pin every `@aws-sdk/*` package to the same exact version. [review]
- A client's region and credentials come from the task, its task role and
  region; never pass keys or set a region in code. [review]
- Wrap each service in one module behind an interface the rest of the code
  depends on; tests fake that interface. [review]
- The SDK retries throttling and transient errors itself; don't add a retry
  loop around it. Catch what you can answer (a failed condition is a 409),
  let the rest reach the `answer`'s 500. [review]

## The load balancer

- Internet-facing, in the public subnets; the tasks are in private subnets
  without public IPs and take traffic only from its security group.
  [review]
- HTTPS on 443 with the deployer's ACM certificate (the `CertificateArn`
  parameter, so synth needs no account), TLS 1.3 and 1.2 only
  (`SslPolicy.RECOMMENDED_TLS`); port 80 only redirects to 443. [review]
- Its security group is open to the internet, which CDK Nag flags: the one
  acknowledged `AwsSolutions-EC23`, on the load balancer. [nag:
  AwsSolutions-EC23]
- Access logs go to an S3 bucket with S3-managed keys (the only encryption
  Elastic Load Balancing writes with), TLS only, objects expiring, through
  the log delivery service principal so the stack keeps no region.
  [nag: AwsSolutions-ELB2, AwsSolutions-S10]
- Invalid header fields are dropped (`dropInvalidHeaderFields`). [review]
- The target group checks `/health`, and its deregistration delay fits the
  longest request: ECS stops a task only after the load balancer has
  drained it. [review]

## The task and the service in the stack

- The image is `ContainerImage.fromAsset` of the repository, for
  `linux/arm64`: synth only stages the build context, which `.dockerignore`
  narrows; `cdk deploy` builds it with Docker on the deployer's machine.
  Never build an image in `make check`, which has no Docker. [review]
- The `Dockerfile` pins the Node major (`node:24-alpine`), builds with
  `npm ci` and esbuild, and runs the bundle alone as the `node` user. [review]
- `arm64` (Graviton): cheaper per vCPU-hour and lower energy. [review]
- CPU and memory set for the work; a read-only root file system; a stop
  timeout that lets requests in flight finish. [review]
- The task role is granted only what the code calls; the execution role
  only what CDK grants it to pull the image and write logs. No AWS managed
  policies. [nag: AwsSolutions-IAM4]
- No wildcard permissions: CDK grants, such as
  `table.grant(taskDefinition.taskRole, 'dynamodb:GetItem')`, never a
  hand-written `*`; the execution role's
  `ecr:GetAuthorizationToken` takes only `*`, and is the one acknowledged
  `AwsSolutions-IAM5[Resource::*]`. [nag: AwsSolutions-IAM5; test: no
  wildcard action]
- No secrets in environment variables: CDK Nag flags every plain variable;
  pass a secret as an ECS `secret` from Secrets Manager. A variable that is
  plainly no secret (a table's name) is acknowledged. [nag: AwsSolutions-ECS2]
- The cluster has Container Insights. [nag: AwsSolutions-ECS4]
- The service keeps at least two tasks in two zones, deploys with the
  circuit breaker and rollback, and scales between bounds the issue's load
  sets. [review]
- The VPC logs rejected traffic to a log group with a retention, and reaches
  DynamoDB through a gateway endpoint. [nag: AwsSolutions-VPC7; review]

## The CDK app

- `infra/main.ts` and `infra/standard.ts` belong to the Standard; the
  Framework's stacks are in `infra/stacks.ts`, which `defineStacks` returns.
  [review]
- Stacks are environment-agnostic: no `env` from `CDK_DEFAULT_ACCOUNT`, no
  `fromLookup`, so synth needs no account. [review]
- Every stack carries the Standard's tags (`c4factory:project`,
  `c4factory:system`, `c4factory:container`). [test: tags every stack]
- Every stateful resource says its removal policy. [test]
- A CDK Nag finding is fixed in the resource. An acknowledgement
  (`Validations.of(construct).acknowledge({ id, reason })`), on the construct
  the finding's path names or an ancestor of it, names one finding
  on one construct, with a reason specific to this system. [review]
- `cdk.json`'s feature flags are the ones `cdk init` recommends for the
  pinned CDK; a task never turns one off. [review]

## Tests

- No test calls AWS, opens a socket or needs credentials. [review]
- Stacks are tested with `Template.fromStack` from `aws-cdk-lib/assertions`:
  `hasResourceProperties`, `resourceCountIs`, `Match`. Assert what the
  issue needs (a route, a grant, a setting), not the whole template. [review]
- Code that calls AWS is tested through its interface with a fake that
  records commands and answers from memory. [review]
- Requests are tested by calling `answer` with a request, or the server's
  listener with an in-memory stream and a recorded response, and checking
  the status code and body; no test starts the server. [review]
