{{ if eq .Framework "aws-ec2-ts" }}## The network and the load balancer

- The VPC has isolated subnets in two zones and no NAT gateway or internet
  gateway: the instances reach S3 (their code, the operating system's
  packages), DynamoDB and CloudWatch Logs through VPC endpoints, and nothing
  else. A new AWS service the code calls needs its endpoint. [test; review]
- The VPC's CIDR is written out (`VPC_CIDR`), so security group rules name
  it and CDK Nag can read them. [nag: AwsSolutions-EC23]
- Flow logs record rejected traffic to a log group with a retention.
  [nag: AwsSolutions-VPC7]
- The load balancer is internal, its security group open only to the VPC on
  its listener's port, and the instances' only to the load balancer on the
  server's port. Making it internet-facing is an issue's decision: it then
  needs HTTPS with a certificate and an authorizer in front of the routes.
  [nag: AwsSolutions-EC23; review]
- It drops invalid header fields and writes access logs to an S3 bucket
  (SSE-S3, TLS only, public access blocked, kept 90 days) that the log
  delivery service may write to. [nag: AwsSolutions-ELB2, AwsSolutions-S10;
  test]
- The target group checks `GET /health` and gives a stopping instance 30
  seconds to finish its requests. [test]

## EC2 instances and the Auto Scaling group

- A launch template, never a launch configuration: Amazon Linux 2023 on
  `arm64` (Graviton: cheaper and lower energy), resolved at deploy time from
  the public SSM parameter, so synth needs no lookup. [review]
- IMDSv2 required, the root volume gp3 and encrypted, detailed monitoring
  on. [nag: AwsSolutions-EC26, AwsSolutions-EC28; test]
- The instances' own role, granted only what the server calls and the
  `s3:GetObject` of its own code; no AWS managed policy, no SSH key, no
  inbound rule but the load balancer's. [nag: AwsSolutions-IAM4,
  AwsSolutions-IAM5; test]
- The server ships as an S3 asset: `infra/server_asset.ts` bundles
  `src/main.ts` with the installed esbuild into one `server.mjs` during
  synth; never Docker. The user data downloads it, installs Node.js and the
  CloudWatch agent from the Amazon Linux repositories, and runs it under
  systemd as its own user. A change to the boot is a change to
  `bootCommands` and its test. [review]
- At least two instances in two zones, a maximum set, a cooldown, a
  target-tracking scaling policy, and the load balancer's health check
  replacing an instance that fails it. [nag: AwsSolutions-AS1,
  AwsSolutions-AS2; test]
- A rolling update replaces one instance at a time and keeps one in
  service. It sends no resource signals (that would need a CloudFormation
  endpoint), so it waits a fixed time per instance and doesn't see whether
  the new one is healthy: a release that can't start replaces every
  instance. Test the boot before a release. [test; review]
- systemd restarts the server whenever it stops, and logrotate keeps its
  log file small on the disk; a change to the boot keeps both. [test]
- No secrets in the user data or the unit's environment: they are visible
  to anyone who can describe the launch template. Read them from Secrets
  Manager at start-up. [review]

{{ else }}{{ if $queue }}## The queue and its event source

- Every queue has a dead-letter queue, after a set number of deliveries
  (`deadLetterQueue: { queue, maxReceiveCount }`), kept 14 days, the
  longest SQS keeps a message. [nag: AwsSolutions-SQS3]
- Every dead-letter queue alarms on its first message: from the stack's
  `Alarms`, `monitorSqsQueueWithDlq` with `addDeadLetterQueueMaxSizeAlarm`.
  [test: the dead-letter queues]
- Queues are encrypted (`QueueEncryption.SQS_MANAGED` or a KMS key) and
  refuse requests without TLS (`enforceSSL: true`). [nag: AwsSolutions-SQS2,
  AwsSolutions-SQS4; test: encrypts every queue]
- The event source reports batch item failures
  (`reportBatchItemFailures: true`), and the handler answers them through
  Powertools' batch processing. [test: every SQS source reports batch item
  failures]
- The queue's visibility timeout is at least six times the function's
  timeout plus the batching window, so a message is not delivered again
  while a batch holds it.
  [review]
- The event source has a maximum concurrency, so a flood of messages
  can't take the account's concurrency from its other functions. [review]
- A queue holds messages not yet consumed, which are data: kept with the
  stack in production, like the tables. [review]

{{ else }}## API Gateway HTTP API

- Every route is authorized: IAM (`HttpIamAuthorizer`), a JWT authorizer or a
  Lambda authorizer. [nag: AwsSolutions-APIG4]
- The stage writes access logs to a log group with a retention.
  [nag: AwsSolutions-APIG1; test]
- The stage is throttled (`throttle: { rateLimit, burstLimit }`), so a burst
  can't run up the bill or starve other functions. [review]
- Routes name their method and path (`GET /items/{id}`); no `ANY /{proxy+}`
  that hands routing to the function. [review]

